1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,550
John Snow, 1855: the first DiD — Cholera, the Thames and two water companies

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:08,450
London, 1849 and 1854. Two water companies, Southwark & Vauxhall and Lambeth, supplied households in the same districts.

3
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:14,950
In 1852 Lambeth moved its intake upstream, above the city’s sewage. Southwark & Vauxhall kept drawing water from the polluted stretch.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:21,950
Cholera death rates: Southwark & Vauxhall went from 135 to 147, Lambeth from 85 to 19.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,950
Lambeth’s change (−66) minus Southwark & Vauxhall’s change (+12) is −78: a difference in differences.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:35,950
Snow (1855) used this comparison to argue that cholera spreads through water, long before germ theory was accepted. Numbers as tabulated in Cunningham (2021).

7
00:00:36,033 --> 00:00:38,583
The 2×2 design — Compare changes, not levels

8
00:00:38,633 --> 00:00:43,483
A policy reaches one group (treated) but not another (control). We observe both groups before and after it starts.

9
00:00:43,533 --> 00:00:49,483
The control group rises from 4.0 to 5.0. That +1.0 reflects everything that changed over time for both groups.

10
00:00:49,533 --> 00:00:55,483
The treated group rises from 6.0 to 8.5. Part of that +2.5 is the common time trend, part is the policy.

11
00:00:55,533 --> 00:01:02,483
Parallel trends: without the policy, the treated group would have risen by the same +1.0 as the control group.

12
00:01:02,533 --> 00:01:08,483
The gap between what happened (8.5) and that counterfactual (7.0) is the average effect on the treated: δ = 1.5.

13
00:01:08,533 --> 00:01:14,983
In numbers: (8.5 − 6.0) − (5.0 − 4.0) = 1.5. The same δ is the interaction coefficient in the regression below.

14
00:01:15,033 --> 00:01:18,983
Stata: didregress (y) (D), group(id) time(t), or reghdfe y D, absorb(id t).

15
00:01:19,067 --> 00:01:21,617
Event studies and parallel trends — One coefficient for every year around treatment

16
00:01:21,667 --> 00:01:28,017
With many periods, estimate one coefficient for each year relative to treatment. Year −1 is the reference and is set to zero.

17
00:01:28,067 --> 00:01:35,017
Leads, the years before treatment, should hover around zero: treated and control units moved together before the policy.

18
00:01:35,067 --> 00:01:42,017
Lags, the years after treatment, trace out how the effect builds up over time.

19
00:01:42,067 --> 00:01:49,017
Caution: flat leads do not prove parallel trends. A small trend like this one passes the pre-test (Roth 2022).

20
00:01:49,067 --> 00:01:55,017
If that trend continued after treatment, part of the estimated effect would be bias rather than policy.

21
00:01:55,067 --> 00:02:01,017
Report the event study, then check how robust it is with Honest DiD bounds (Rambachan & Roth 2023).

22
00:02:01,100 --> 00:02:03,650
Triple differences — Difference out a shock that hits the treated states

23
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:10,050
Worry: the treated states may have had their own shock, unrelated to the policy, that a plain DiD would mistake for an effect.

24
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:18,050
Step 1: a standard DiD for the group the policy targets. Treated vs. control states, before vs. after: 3.0 − 1.0 = 2.0.

25
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:26,050
Step 2: the same DiD for a group in the same states that the policy does not touch. Its 0.5 measures the state-specific shock.

26
00:02:26,100 --> 00:02:33,050
Step 3: subtract. The shock cancels and the policy effect remains: 2.0 − 0.5 = 1.5.

27
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:40,050
Only the gap in trends between the two groups has to be parallel, a weaker assumption (Gruber 1994; Olden & Møen 2022).

28
00:02:40,133 --> 00:02:42,683
Conditional parallel trends — When the groups differ in who they contain

29
00:02:42,733 --> 00:02:49,583
Sometimes trends are only parallel within comparable units. Here young workers’ outcomes grow faster than older workers’, policy or not.

30
00:02:49,633 --> 00:02:55,083
The treated group is 80% young; the control group is only 20% young.

31
00:02:55,133 --> 00:03:02,583
A raw DiD compares groups with different mixes, so it mistakes the faster young trend for an effect: 2.7 − 0.8 = 1.9.

32
00:03:02,633 --> 00:03:10,583
Instead compare treated and control units within each age group, then average with the treated group’s mix: the effect is 1.0.

33
00:03:10,633 --> 00:03:19,083
Doubly robust DiD combines an outcome model with propensity-score weights and is consistent if either one is right (Sant’Anna & Zhao 2020).

34
00:03:19,167 --> 00:03:21,717
Staggered adoption: why TWFE breaks — Already-treated units make bad controls

35
00:03:21,767 --> 00:03:28,117
Now units adopt at different dates: an early cohort in year 4, a late cohort in year 7, and a group that is never treated.

36
00:03:28,167 --> 00:03:34,117
Effects build up after adoption (gold). Static two-way fixed effects (TWFE) uses all of these data and returns one number.

37
00:03:34,167 --> 00:03:39,117
Goodman-Bacon (2021): that number is a weighted average of every 2×2 comparison hidden in the data.

38
00:03:39,167 --> 00:03:45,117
Early vs. never treated, and late vs. never treated: clean comparisons.

39
00:03:45,167 --> 00:03:50,117
Early vs. late, before the late cohort is treated: also clean, because the late cohort is still untreated.

40
00:03:50,167 --> 00:03:57,117
Late vs. early, after year 4: the early cohort serves as the control while its own effect is still growing.

41
00:03:57,167 --> 00:04:03,117
Its growth gets subtracted, so this comparison gives −0.50 even though every true effect is positive.

42
00:04:03,167 --> 00:04:09,117
Result: TWFE reports 1.14, while the true average effect on the treated is 1.73.

43
00:04:09,167 --> 00:04:16,117
Diagnose with bacondecomp or twowayfeweights. Fix it with estimators that only use clean controls, as in the next scenes.

44
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:18,750
Callaway & Sant’Anna — Many clean 2×2s, one for each cohort and year

45
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:25,150
Callaway & Sant’Anna (2021) split the problem into many clean 2×2 comparisons: one for each cohort g and year t.

46
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:33,150
ATT(g,t) compares cohort g’s change since its last untreated year, g−1, with the same change for never-treated or not-yet-treated units.

47
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:40,150
Example: ATT(4,6) takes cohort 4’s change from year 3 to year 6, minus the never-treated group’s change: 2.25 − 0.75 = 1.5.

48
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:47,150
Repeat for every cohort and year. An already-treated unit is never used as a control.

49
00:04:47,200 --> 00:04:55,150
Then aggregate. Averaging the cells with the same time since adoption gives an event study…

50
00:04:55,200 --> 00:05:01,150
…or a single overall ATT that weights cohorts by size: 1.73, exactly the true average effect.

51
00:05:01,200 --> 00:05:06,150
Stata: csdid (doubly robust by default), or the official xthdidregress in Stata 18 and later.

52
00:05:06,233 --> 00:05:08,783
Sun & Abraham — Event studies one cohort at a time

53
00:05:08,833 --> 00:05:16,183
A pooled event-study regression mixes cohorts. With heterogeneous effects, leads and lags pick up effects from other horizons (Sun & Abraham 2021).

54
00:05:16,233 --> 00:05:23,183
Here the pooled regression (grey) shows a pre-trend, although nobody anticipated the policy, and it distorts the effects after adoption.

55
00:05:23,233 --> 00:05:30,183
Fix: estimate a separate event study for each cohort, using only a clean control cohort such as the never treated.

56
00:05:30,233 --> 00:05:37,183
Then average across cohorts at each horizon, weighting by each cohort’s share of the units observed at that horizon: here 40% and 60%.

57
00:05:37,233 --> 00:05:42,183
The interaction-weighted estimate (gold) is flat before adoption and recovers the average effect after it.

58
00:05:42,233 --> 00:05:46,183
Stata: eventstudyinteract.

59
00:05:46,267 --> 00:05:48,817
Imputation and two-stage DiD — Predict the untreated outcome, then compare

60
00:05:48,867 --> 00:05:55,217
Imputation estimators (Borusyak, Jaravel & Spiess 2024) start from the observations that are not yet treated: the filled dots.

61
00:05:55,267 --> 00:06:02,217
Step 1: fit unit and year fixed effects using only those untreated observations.

62
00:06:02,267 --> 00:06:09,217
Step 2: extend the fit into the treated years to impute each unit’s outcome without treatment, Y(0).

63
00:06:09,267 --> 00:06:16,217
Step 3: each treated observation’s effect is its observed outcome minus the imputed Y(0).

64
00:06:16,267 --> 00:06:23,217
Average these gaps by years since adoption, or overall. Treated observations never serve as controls.

65
00:06:23,267 --> 00:06:30,217
Gardner’s two-stage DiD (did2s) and Wooldridge’s extended TWFE (jwdid) give the same point estimates in this setting.

66
00:06:30,300 --> 00:06:32,850
Stacked DiD — One clean experiment per adoption date

67
00:06:32,900 --> 00:06:38,250
Stacked DiD builds one clean experiment for each adoption date, then pools them. Coloured cells are treated years.

68
00:06:38,300 --> 00:06:43,250
Take the year-4 cohort and a window from 2 years before to 2 years after adoption.

69
00:06:43,300 --> 00:06:50,250
Add controls that stay untreated throughout that window: the year-8 cohort and the never treated. The year-6 cohort is dropped.

70
00:06:50,300 --> 00:06:57,250
Do the same for the year-6 and year-8 cohorts, each with its own clean controls.

71
00:06:57,300 --> 00:07:03,250
Align the stacks on event time and pile them into one dataset.

72
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:12,250
Estimate one regression with stack-specific unit and time fixed effects (Cengiz et al. 2019). Wing, Freedman & Hollingsworth (2024) add weights so it targets a clear average.

73
00:07:12,333 --> 00:07:14,883
Local-projections DiD — One long-difference regression per horizon

74
00:07:14,933 --> 00:07:21,283
Local-projections DiD (Dube, Girardi, Jordà & Taylor 2023) runs one simple regression for each horizon h after adoption.

75
00:07:21,333 --> 00:07:28,283
For horizon h, take the long difference from the year before adoption to h years after: y(t+h) − y(t−1).

76
00:07:28,333 --> 00:07:36,283
Compare newly treated units with clean controls: units not yet treated by year t+h. The late cohort drops out once it adopts in year 7.

77
00:07:36,333 --> 00:07:43,283
Each regression adds one point to the event study, and no already-treated unit is ever used as a control.

78
00:07:43,333 --> 00:07:50,283
It is fast on large data and easy to extend with covariates and non-absorbing treatments. Stata: lpdid.

79
00:07:50,367 --> 00:07:52,917
Treatments that switch on, off or change — de Chaisemartin & D’Haultfœuille

80
00:07:52,967 --> 00:07:59,817
Many treatments are not one-time, on-forever switches. They can turn off, or change in intensity. Each row is a unit, each column a year.

81
00:07:59,867 --> 00:08:06,317
de Chaisemartin & D’Haultfœuille compare switchers with units that had the same treatment at the start and have not switched yet.

82
00:08:06,367 --> 00:08:14,317
Example: unit 1 switches on in year 4. Its controls are the units still at its starting level, 0: units 2, 3, 5 and 6. Unit 4 started treated, so it is excluded.

83
00:08:14,367 --> 00:08:22,317
Effects are traced ℓ = 1, 2, … periods after the first switch, as long as not-yet-switched controls remain. The control set shrinks as units switch.

84
00:08:22,367 --> 00:08:30,317
Placebos compare the same groups before the switch. Stata: did_multiplegt_dyn; for designs where everyone gets some dose, did_had.

85
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:32,950
Continuous treatment — When units get different doses

86
00:08:33,000 --> 00:08:39,350
With a continuous treatment, units receive different doses: here 0, 1, 2 or 3. Bars show each dose group’s change in the outcome.

87
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:47,350
Under ordinary parallel trends, each dose group’s change minus the untreated group’s change identifies the effect of that dose on the units that received it.

88
00:08:47,400 --> 00:08:55,350
Comparing across doses is harder. The jump from dose 2 to dose 3 (0.9) also reflects differences between the units that ended up with those doses.

89
00:08:55,400 --> 00:09:03,350
For the dose-3 units themselves the marginal gain is only 0.6. Reading the curve as a causal dose-response needs “strong” parallel trends (Callaway, Goodman-Bacon & Sant’Anna 2024).

90
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:09,350
If every unit gets some dose there is no untreated group at all; did_had uses units with small doses instead (de Chaisemartin et al. 2024).

91
00:09:09,433 --> 00:09:11,983
Fuzzy DiD — When only the share treated changes

92
00:09:12,033 --> 00:09:18,383
Sometimes no group is fully treated or untreated. Take-up just rises faster in one group than in another.

93
00:09:18,433 --> 00:09:25,883
Share treated: group A goes from 20% to 60%, group B from 20% to 30%. The first-stage DiD is 40 − 10 = 30 percentage points.

94
00:09:25,933 --> 00:09:32,383
Outcomes: group A rises by 1.6, group B by 0.4. The outcome DiD is 1.2.

95
00:09:32,433 --> 00:09:39,383
Divide them: the Wald-DiD is 1.2 / 0.30 = 4.0, the effect per unit of treatment, much like an instrumental-variables ratio.

96
00:09:39,433 --> 00:09:47,383
It needs more than parallel trends; de Chaisemartin & D’Haultfœuille (2018) also propose time-corrected and changes-in-changes versions. Stata: fuzzydid.

97
00:09:47,467 --> 00:09:50,017
Changes-in-changes — The whole counterfactual distribution, not just the mean

98
00:09:50,067 --> 00:09:56,417
DiD compares averages. Changes-in-changes (Athey & Imbens 2006) recovers the whole counterfactual distribution.

99
00:09:56,467 --> 00:10:03,417
In the control group, see how each rank of the distribution moved over time: the 10th, 50th and 90th percentiles.

100
00:10:03,467 --> 00:10:10,917
Give each treated unit the change that a control unit with the same before-period outcome experienced.

101
00:10:10,967 --> 00:10:18,417
Compare the actual treated distribution with this counterfactual: there is an effect at every quantile, larger at the top.

102
00:10:18,467 --> 00:10:26,417
Unlike DiD, the answer does not depend on whether the outcome is measured in levels or logs. Stata: cic.

103
00:10:26,500 --> 00:10:29,050
Synthetic control and synthetic DiD — Build a comparison unit that tracks the treated one

104
00:10:29,100 --> 00:10:35,950
One treated unit and a pool of potential controls. Their simple average (dashed) does not follow the treated unit before treatment.

105
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:44,450
Synthetic control chooses weights on control units so that their weighted average tracks the treated unit before treatment (Abadie et al. 2010).

106
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:51,450
After treatment, the gap between the treated unit and its synthetic twin is the estimated effect.

107
00:10:51,500 --> 00:10:59,450
Synthetic DiD (Arkhangelsky et al. 2021) also weights the pre-treatment years and allows a level shift, so it only has to match trends, not levels.

108
00:10:59,500 --> 00:11:06,450
Useful with few treated units when no comparison group has parallel trends on its own. Stata: sdid, synth.

109
00:11:06,533 --> 00:11:09,083
Honest DiD — How big a violation would overturn the result?

110
00:11:09,133 --> 00:11:15,983
Parallel trends cannot be tested after treatment. Honest DiD asks how large a violation would overturn the result (Rambachan & Roth 2023).

111
00:11:16,033 --> 00:11:21,983
Start from the largest jump between consecutive pre-treatment coefficients: here 0.20.

112
00:11:22,033 --> 00:11:28,983
Allow each post-treatment jump in the violation to be up to M̄ times that size. The cone shows what M̄ = 1 permits.

113
00:11:29,033 --> 00:11:37,983
As M̄ grows, the robust confidence interval for the average effect widens. In this illustration it first includes zero near M̄ = 1.1: the breakdown value.

114
00:11:38,033 --> 00:11:44,483
Report this sensitivity plot next to the event study. Stata: honestdid; pretrends checks the power of pre-tests.

115
00:11:44,567 --> 00:11:47,117
Serial correlation and clustering — How often does DiD find a law that does not exist?

116
00:11:47,167 --> 00:11:53,517
Bertrand, Duflo & Mullainathan (2004) asked how often DiD finds an effect of a law that does not exist.

117
00:11:53,567 --> 00:12:00,517
Here outcomes in each of 50 states drift slowly over 20 years (serial correlation 0.8). Each dot will be one placebo law, given at random to 25 states.

118
00:12:00,567 --> 00:12:08,517
With conventional standard errors, 39% of the 200 placebo laws look significant at the 5% level.

119
00:12:08,567 --> 00:12:16,017
Clustering standard errors by state allows for correlation over time within a state. The rejection rate falls to 5%.

120
00:12:16,067 --> 00:12:22,517
Rule: cluster at the level at which treatment is assigned, for example vce(cluster state) in Stata.

121
00:12:22,600 --> 00:12:25,150
Few clusters: the wild cluster bootstrap — When even clustered standard errors are too small

122
00:12:25,200 --> 00:12:31,550
With few clusters, or few treated clusters, even clustered standard errors are too small. Here: 12 states, 3 of them treated.

123
00:12:31,600 --> 00:12:39,050
The wild cluster bootstrap redraws the data many times. Each state’s residuals are kept or flipped in sign as one block.

124
00:12:39,100 --> 00:12:46,550
Take one placebo dataset where the two tests disagree: the clustered t-statistic is −2.24, “significant” by the usual rule, but the share of bootstrap t-statistics at least as extreme gives p = 0.055.

125
00:12:46,600 --> 00:12:54,550
Across 300 simulated placebo laws, clustered standard errors reject 8.3% of the time instead of 5%; the wild cluster bootstrap rejects 5%.

126
00:12:54,600 --> 00:13:02,550
Stata: boottest right after your regression (Roodman et al. 2019). See Cameron, Gelbach & Miller (2008) and MacKinnon & Webb (2017).
